sclip(1) -- sanitize clipboard-bound text to safe characters
============================================================

<!--
# Copyright (C) 2026 - 2026 ENCRYPTED SUPPORT LLC <adrelanos@whonix.org>
# See the file COPYING for copying conditions.
-->

## SYNOPSIS

`sclip` [`--unicode`] [`--summary`]

## DESCRIPTION

sclip reads text on standard input and writes clipboard-safe text on standard
output. It is a pure filter -- it opens no files, needs no display and never
touches the clipboard itself -- so it composes with the system clipboard tools
and confines tightly under AppArmor:

    wl-paste | sclip | wl-copy
    xclip -o | sclip | xclip -i

The purpose is to make text safe to paste into an editor, or any program that
does not neutralize Unicode: invisible, zero-width, bidirectional-control and
other control characters are removed.

By default sclip is STRICTEST: it keeps only printable ASCII plus tab and
newline, so a cross-script homoglyph (a look-alike posing as ASCII) cannot ride
out either.

The `st-wl-paste`(1), `st-wl-copy`(1) and `st-xclip`(1) drop-in wrappers run
these pipelines for you.

## OPTIONS

  * `--unicode`:
    Keep printable non-ASCII characters (accents, CJK, ...) instead of dropping
    them. Note the honest limit: a homoglyph IS printable Unicode, so `--unicode`
    does NOT remove look-alikes; it removes only the invisible, bidirectional and
    control classes. Use the default, or the review GUI (`secure-terminal
    --clipboard-watch`), when look-alikes matter.

  * `--summary`:
    Write a one-line, mode-accurate report of what was removed (and, in
    `--unicode` mode, what printable non-ASCII was kept) to standard error.
    Standard output is unchanged.

## EXIT STATUS

0 on success; 1 on an unreadable input or a closed output pipe; 2 on an unknown
option.

## SEE ALSO

`st-wl-paste`(1), `st-wl-copy`(1), `st-xclip`(1), `secure-terminal`(1),
